#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a369d9b2-7637-5339-bd75-5f49b1aac962" :question/math-academy-id "e-9842" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}$." :question/worked-solution "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{x^{3}}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x^{3}\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (x^{3})^{′} \\cdot (\\ln x) + (x^{3}) \\cdot (\\ln x)^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 3x^{2} \\cdot \\ln x + x^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=x^{x^3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{x^{3}}(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) \\\\\n&= x^{x^{3}}(x^{2}(3\\ln x + 1)) \\\\\n&= x^{x^{3} + 2}(3\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/id #uuid "bdccebc1-05df-5c7b-a57b-fa50df42a32e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9843" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x$." :question/worked-solution "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((x^{2} - 1)^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x\\ln (x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln (x^{2} - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln (x^{2} - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \\cdot \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 1} \\cdot 2x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= y(\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (x^{2} - 1)^{x}(\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 1)^{x}(((x^{2} - 1)\\ln (x^{2} - 1))/(x^{2} - 1) + \\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1}) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 1)^{x - 1}((x^{2} - 1)\\ln (x^{2} - 1) + 2x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "4d047c1a-308b-553b-89e4-9afc5cc9f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9844" :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}$." :question/worked-solution "We have the function\n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((\\sin x)^{\\cos x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\cos x\\ln (\\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x\\ln (\\sin x)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (\\cos x)^{′} \\cdot (\\ln (\\sin x)) + (\\cos x) \\cdot (\\ln (\\sin x))^{′} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= (-\\sin x) \\cdot \\ln (\\sin x) + \\cos x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\cos x) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}(\\cos x\\cot x - \\sin x\\ln (\\sin x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11871c64-6368-59c5-9ec4-a19994f69e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410216} {:answer/id #uuid "543a59b8-c1fa-5f13-aee5-fec0cb00f970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410262} {:answer/id #uuid "80147df6-2320-55c9-944a-766dbd01c6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410284} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2b317b-6389-5a05-8b28-b44cfeff3161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410304} {:answer/id #uuid "b4326574-8b69-5df3-9063-abf1241c8efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80147df6-2320-55c9-944a-766dbd01c6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410284} :answer-field/id #uuid "19581f53-386c-55c5-88ff-b5d2b3116bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29625a08-0232-5ce8-97e1-f2bb9cf1a9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-138856" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-5x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862416fc-8262-5750-8307-92410b956ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061946} {:answer/id #uuid "90d05d15-8894-5134-ada1-2d7eda3890bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061947} {:answer/id #uuid "d621e073-81c9-5f51-9eb7-aefc2f7cbd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ln (2x) + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061948} {:answer/id #uuid "312dceec-6efb-5819-83aa-0f14501a1f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (2x) + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061949} {:answer/id #uuid "541a2df1-ac1d-5307-b422-691a056ddb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + \\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061950}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1b873f0e-d503-59f0-bb98-854722b44541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061945}] :question/id #uuid "e49f218d-0bc2-5311-beb0-0c74453b8fc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2519" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = (2x)^{x}$. Its derivative is given by\n\n$y^{′} = (2x)^{x} \\cdot g(x)$.\n\nFind the function $g(x)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94a2b8cb-aca4-5e18-bb5c-71c0738c9e30" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e24a86b9-7405-5dbf-acfb-1af04c6cd34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24a86b9-7405-5dbf-acfb-1af04c6cd34f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413617} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f8ee9f-a89b-56b6-9a08-fe333af36907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d2d4364-ca99-526d-979f-b7f7eb9eac46" :question/math-academy-id "q-341945" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,{y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2x+1})+({x}^{2}+1)\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+1}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\,(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\sqrt{2x+1})}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=0} & ={(\\sqrt{2(0)+1})}^{(0)^{2}+1}(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)+1)+\\frac{(0)^{2}+1}{2(0)+1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
